  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA stalling frontal boundary is moving into the Finger Lakes \nregion this afternoon with mainly light rain along the front. \nWith some additional day time heating, better surface based CAPE\nfor the Southern Tier into NEPA will allow for a few \nthunderstorms to develop. Shear along the front is around 30 \nknots so if a storm can get tall enough to get into the higher \nwinds above around 10 thousand feet, some small hail would be \npossible. Low level lapse rates and good humidity aloft will \nmake it difficult for storms to develop a strong downburst so \nsevere storms are unlikely. Tonight with all the low level \nmoisture in place and some clearing at times, widespread valley \nfog development is expected once again. \n\nTomorrow, the front that is moving through today looks to stall\nsomewhere in central PA. An area of low pressure propagating \nalong the front will produce a strip of heavy rain along its \nwest to east path. There is uncertainty with how far south the \nfront will stall but it could be draped across a portion of \nNEPA. If it does, 1 to 2 inches of rain is possible for the \nWyoming Valley into the Poconos. Even though rainfall rates dont\nlook to be too high, runoff rates will be higher than typical \ndue to recent rains so some smaller streams and tributaries may \napproach bank full. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \n\nOnce the weak surface low moves east of the region tomorrow \nevening, the front will continue its progress south with high \npressure building in. Morning fog will be commonplace with the \nlonger nights and clear skies on Friday and Saturday. A long \nwave trough digs in late Saturday into Sunday. Late Saturday \ninto Sunday has some uncertainty with the rain coverage as the \n500 mb trough is trying to become negatively tilted. Some of the\nmodels have a stronger slow moving low near the Atlantic coast \nwith an area of heavier rain on the NW side of the low. Trends \nhave been bringing some of this rain into NEPA and even into the\nCatskills. If this low stays to the south, only afternoon \nshowers are expected as cooler air moves in aloft helping lead \nto afternoon destabilization with day time heating. The Great \nLakes are warm enough with the 850 mb temperatures falling to \naround 5 to 10C to get some lake effect rain early next week.",
